{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-7-013-spring-2013", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["7.013"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Introductory Biology", "course_slug": "7-013-introductory-biology-spring-2013", "term": "Spring", "year": 2013, "as_taught_in": "Spring 2013", "language": "en", "departments": ["Biology"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Hazel Sive", "Prof. Tyler Jacks", "Dr. Diviya Sinha"], "subject_primary": "Biology", "subject_secondary": ["Biochemistry", "Cell Biology", "Developmental Biology", "Genetics", "Molecular Biology"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Biology", "specialties": ["Biochemistry", "Cell Biology", "Developmental Biology", "Genetics", "Molecular Biology"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Exam Solutions", "Exams", "Instructor Insights", "Lecture Videos", "Problem Set Solutions", "Problem Sets"], "description": "The MIT Biology Department core courses, 7.012 , 7.013, and 7.014 , all cover the same core material, which includes the fundamental principles of biochemistry, genetics, molecular biology, and cell biology. 7.013 focuses on the application of the fundamental principles toward an understanding of human biology. Topics include genetics, cell biology, molecular biology, disease (infectious agents, inherited diseases and cancer), developmental biology, neurobiology and evolution. Biological function at the molecular level is particularly emphasized in all courses and covers the structure and regulation of genes, as well as, the structure and synthesis of proteins, how these molecules are integrated into cells, and how these cells are integrated into multicellular systems and organisms. In addition, each version of the subject has its own distinctive material.", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/7-0